{"name":"Zero Degrees","hex":"#ddd9c4","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"zero","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["NATO & ICAO radiotelephony clear code (spelling-alphabet name) for the digit 0."]},{"part":"Numeral","definitions":["The cardinal number occurring before one and that denotes no quantity or amount at all, represented in Arabic numerals as 0."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/zero"},{"term":"degrees","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of degree"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/degrees"}],"entries":[{"title":"0","blocks":[{"text":"0 is a number representing an empty quantity. Adding 0 to any number leaves that number unchanged; in mathematical terminology, 0 is the additive identity of the integers, rational numbers, real numbers, and complex numbers, as well as other algebraic structures. Multiplying any number by 0 results in 0, and consequently dividing by 0 is generally considered to be undefined in arithmetic. Additionally, any number raised to the power of 0 is equal to 1, with exception of 0 to the power of 0 which is sometimes undefined."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/0"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/P._Lund%2C_Inv._35a.jpg/500px-P._Lund%2C_Inv._35a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/P._Lund%2C_Inv._35a.jpg/500px-P._Lund%2C_Inv._35a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Example of the early Greek symbol for zero (lower right corner) from a 2nd-century papyrus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AP._Lund%2C_Inv._35a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Zero_in_Rod_Calculus.png/500px-Zero_in_Rod_Calculus.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.o